Under BNSS § 35(1)(b), a police officer may arrest without a warrant for a cognizable offence punishable with imprisonment up to seven years **only if**, in addition to having a reasonable complaint, credible information, or reasonable suspicion, the officer **is satisfied that the arrest is necessary** for one of the following purposes:

- to prevent the person from committing any further offence;  
- for proper investigation of the offence;  
- to prevent the person from causing the evidence of the offence to disappear or tampering with such evidence;  
- to prevent the person from making any inducement, threat, or promise to anyone acquainted with the facts of the case so as to dissuade them from disclosing those facts to the Court or police; or  
- unless the person is arrested, his presence in Court whenever required cannot be ensured.  

The officer must also record the reasons for the arrest in writing【5e158157-c2be-4da0-a502-178c54007990】.
